<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CycloneDDS>
  <Domain>
    <General>
      <!-- For the robot (Galactic, Cyclone DDS 0.22.6) ONLY -- mars
           (Jazzy, newer Cyclone DDS) uses cyclonedds_jazzy.xml instead.

           spdp, not false, and NO 127.0.0.1 peer: unlike Jazzy's Cyclone
           DDS, adding 127.0.0.1 as an explicit static peer on this older
           Galactic build made same-host discovery WORSE, not better --
           confirmed: controller spawners went from occasionally succeeding
           to failing 100% of the time (5/5 retries) with "empty node name
           returned by the RMW layer" once 127.0.0.1 was added. Likely this
           version's peer-discovery code doesn't handle a self-referential
           unicast peer cleanly. spdp (permit multicast for SPDP discovery
           only, never for data) is the best known option here: it isn't
           fully reliable for same-host discovery either (this Galactic
           Cyclone DDS build has a documented, unfixed upstream discovery-
           timing race -- see the retrying_spawner wrapper in
           real_robot_hardware.launch.py, which is the actual mitigation),
           but it's strictly better than 127.0.0.1 here. Cross-host
           discovery (mars<->robot) still goes through the static Peers
           list below regardless -- the campus Wi-Fi blocks multicast for
           that regardless of this setting. -->
      <AllowMulticast>spdp</AllowMulticast>
    </General>
    <Discovery>
      <Peers>
        <Peer address="172.30.6.165"/>
        <Peer address="172.27.89.157"/>
      </Peers>
    </Discovery>
    <Internal>
      <!-- Confirmed on real hardware (2026-07-26, late-night session):
           EVERY test run tonight -- across several different scripts,
           trajectory sizes, and completion-detection mechanisms -- breaks
           in the exact same way after almost exactly 3 successful action
           goals (regardless of how much wall-clock time those 3 goals
           spanned, from ~4s to several minutes across different runs):
           robot_state_publisher AND ros2_control_node both start emitting
           repeated serdata.cpp:354 deserialization errors simultaneously,
           and no further goal is ever received again for the rest of that
           process's lifetime -- this is a hard, count-triggered failure,
           not a probabilistic network drop (ruled out fragmentation/size
           as the cause: single ~500-byte goals hit this exactly as often
           as 30-waypoint ~9KB ones). This is consistent with a small,
           default Cyclone DDS writer/reader history or resource-limit
           high-water-mark being reached after a handful of samples with
           no support for recovering once hit, rather than a lossy-link
           packet-drop issue. Raising the watermark gives the writer more
           room before whatever backpressure/limit mechanism kicks in.
           UNVERIFIED as of writing -- this is a targeted guess based on
           the count-not-time pattern, not a confirmed root cause; if this
           doesn't resolve it, the next step is asking upstream
           (cyclonedds/rmw_cyclonedds issue trackers) about this exact
           "N goals then permanent serdata.cpp:354" signature on 0.22.6. -->
      <Watermarks>
        <WhcHigh>500kB</WhcHigh>
      </Watermarks>
    </Internal>
  </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
